### Scope

- For continuous modes on compact Borel \(X\) with finite \(\mu\), apply the parent’s `integrable_continuous_compact` to each mode. Apply it again on \(X\times X\) with \(\mu\otimes\mu\) to obtain `hjoint`. The joint integrand is continuous by continuity of the projections, conjugation, multiplication, and finite sums.
- If the actual density relative to \(\mu\otimes\mu\) is \(K(x,y)=\sum_r a_r\overline{\phi_r(x)}\phi_r(y)\), then \(\theta(x,y)=(y,x)\) and \(F_+(x,y)=F(y)\) give the tested integrand \(\overline{F(x)}F(y)K(x,y)\). Distributing those factors gives the Gram integrand with \(u_r=\phi_r F\). For the normalized measure, establish \(Z>0\) separately and scale the integral by \(1/Z\).
- Finite Gram approximants can have negative pointwise values, so their quadratic-form positivity alone supplies no probability density. Applying this result to the infinite Wilson character expansion requires convergence of the tested integrals to those for the actual positive Wilson density. The compact continuous matrix-coefficient expansion for \(\beta\ge0\) is a separate analytic application.

